## Design questions the pass must settle
1. When things are recorded. What a field is, where it is defined and its description are recorded at define time.
   Where it is used is NOT: an action's body is built on its first run. An action that has never run shows no uses,
   unless a search forces the build or there is a parse-only pass.
2. Two meanings of "used": the name as written in a body, or the field it resolves to through the search list (which is
   decided at run time). The index says which one it reports.
3. The facts do not live on the field. They leak into every copy (the Reference recon measured this). They go in
   registries keyed by name, the way fORMfACTs does it.
4. Delete and redefine are one job. An overwrite (last wins) retracts the old uses exactly as a delete does. A delete
   rule is needed, the inverse of define. The tester checks the round trip.
5. The switch gets its own flag, not noPrint. Typesetting and the jitter both read it.
6. Jitter and the switch: bake it in at emit and rejit when it flips (costs nothing while off, which matters for probes),
   or emit a run-time check and never rejit. Unmeasured. Known: a second testing() on one action corrupted state, hence
   jitRefire. Is a rejit mid-run safe, or only once per run?
 
